a  there were actually two other people  that i had met  they all said that they gone to this  freelance website for programmers  and clients from around the world would  come  and give them orders give them jobs to  do  some of the jobs given to zhang were  cracking open  commercially successful anti-virus  programs  and once he was able to open that up he  replicated that program  into another product which his client  would  repackage as a whole different product  to be sold to other people he also  created  what we now call bots there are very  popular online games  including lineage he would create  a buff that would just roam around  without  any supervision from the user  and basically trade items with other  users  on that game he would throw that bot on  lineage and sell it for  almost a hundred dollars another  defector said the money from clients was  wired primarily via paypal  to accounts controlled by minders  because they were the only ones with  access to legitimate bank accounts  john had to make at least a hundred  thousand dollars a year  and he doesn't get to keep most of it  actually most of the money goes the  minders  to the north korean regime but if you  don't meet the quota  and the minders will make sure that  they'll put enough pressure on you  and if that happens repeatedly you will  face the  growing odds of being sent back home  among the three people that i spoke to  there's a common thing that they all  tell me which is that  on every saturday they have to do this  propaganda session which  is basically about gathering all the  hackers together  into a room and they have to recite  whatever messages whatever lines come  from  pyongyang about the kin family about  socialism  if you're especially someone who with a  free set of mind  that's stressful one person actually was  really stressed out  what he heard about north korea once he  was in china  during the propaganda sessions he just  couldn't take  [Music]  john was a senior member of the unit  that he belonged to  and he was good at chinese as well  whenever  a senior north korean official came from  north korea  to check on the hacking units in china  john would accompany that official he  doesn't  provide me details but he calls it  unsavory incident that happened between  him  and an official that he was traveling  with  and that altercation prompted him  to defect but  there was no one that he could turn to  after the facting  he would in disguise roam around china  he told me that he actually enjoyed it  he actually enjoyed the freedom of  you know not being known to anybody just  roaming around  a country that was huge  how did he make the money that he needed  to carry on  he did the same thing he did you know at  the north korean unit he he would take  orders he would reverse engineer  software  to make money but at one point  he said he was just sick and tired of it  all of a sudden he just  he wanted to find a place where where he  could just settle down  and he contacted south korean  authorities  now he lives in korea he met a woman  here  you know last time i checked they had a  baby  he worked for a software company  ironically  an anti-virus company  what keeps coming up in my head  even long after i spoke to these people  is there was one common thing about them  which was the sense of disillusionment  once they had  access to outside information once they  were able to  freely roam in the internet  there was a spark  and once they realized that there is a  discrepancy between what they think is  really happening and  what north korea is telling them every  saturday morning  that's when the spark gets bigger  [Music]  the little fire is what  eventually drives them to decide to  leave  even if it takes a huge risk on their  [Applause]  lives  [Music]  you

